    "problem_statement": "Duration formatting produces incorrect ISO 8601 strings\n\n#### Description\n\nWhen formatting Duration objects to ISO 8601 strings, the output is malformed and doesn't match the expected format. Years and months appear to be swapped, time components are incorrect, and the separator character is wrong.\n\n#### Steps/Code to Reproduce\n\n```python\nfrom isodate import Duration, duration_isoformat\n\n# Create a duration with years and months\ndur = Duration(years=18, months=9, days=4, hours=11, minutes=9, seconds=8)\nresult = duration_isoformat(dur, 'P%P')\nprint(f\"Got: {result}\")\nprint(f\"Expected: P18Y9M4DT11H9M8S\")\n\n# Another example with just years\ndur2 = Duration(years=4)\nresult2 = duration_isoformat(dur2, 'P%P')\nprint(f\"Got: {result2}\")\nprint(f\"Expected: P4Y\")\n\n# Example with weeks\nfrom datetime import timedelta\ntd = timedelta(weeks=2)\nresult3 = duration_isoformat(td, 'P%p')\nprint(f\"Got: {result3}\")\nprint(f\"Expected: P2W\")\n```\n\nThe output shows malformed ISO strings where:\n- Years and months positions are swapped (Y and M suffixes appear with wrong values)\n- Time separator is \"Z\" instead of \"T\"\n- Week calculations are incorrect\n- Microsecond formatting is wrong\n- Default empty duration returns \"P1D\" instead of \"P0D\"\n\nThis affects any code that relies on proper ISO 8601 duration formatting for serialization, APIs, or data exchange."
  }
